.TH TSORT 1 
.SH NAME
tsort \- topological sort
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tsort
[ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tsort
produces on the standard output a totally ordered list of items
consistent with a partial ordering of items
mentioned in the input
.IR file .
If no
.I file
is specified, the standard input is understood.
.PP
The input consists of pairs of items (nonempty strings)
separated by blanks.
Pairs of different items indicate ordering.
Pairs of identical items indicate presence, but not ordering.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
lorder(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Odd data: there is an odd number of fields in the input file.
.br
.B "tsort: name too long: ..."
\-
an item longer than 255 bytes.
.I Note:
255 is
.IR ar (1)'s
longest member name on this machine, which is what
.IR lorder (1)
feeds this program;
v7 read items into a fifty-byte buffer with no bound at all.
.br
.B "tsort: cycle in reverse order"
followed by the items in the cycle, on the standard error output.
The ordering is still printed.
.SH "EIGHT BITS"
An item is any run of bytes with no blank, tab, newline, carriage return, form
feed or vertical tab in it, so a byte above 0177 is an ordinary item character
and a Cyrillic name sorts like any other.
.SH BUGS
Uses a quadratic algorithm;
not worth fixing for the typical use of ordering
a library archive file.
